From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 11:21:26 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] venus: bound device memory by a host-stated share

A Venus guest was shown the host driver's whole device heap. On a
unified-memory host that heap is most of the system: an iPad mini 6
reports a recommended working set of 2730 MiB against an app limit of
2864 MiB. Every byte of it is also charged to the same process as the
guest's own RAM, so a guest sizing its textures from that heap could
take the whole application down.

Let the host state how much device memory the guest may use, as a pool
with a limit and callbacks: how much is allocated or reserved now, a
reservation, and its release. Every heap the guest is shown is at most
that limit, memory budgets leave the guest only what the pool has left,
and a new allocation the pool cannot reserve fails with
VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DEVICE_MEMORY, which an application is prepared to
handle. An import names memory that already exists and is not charged.
A host that states no limit keeps today's behaviour.

The renderer reserves an allocation's size before the driver makes it
and releases the reservation once the driver has made it or failed, so
allocations the renderer admits on different threads never pass the
limit together, which a check that only read the host's allocation
counter allowed. The driver also allocates memory for itself that no
guest allocation accounts for, KosmicKrisp's geometry heap among it, so
every device the renderer creates is given the same pool through
KosmicKrisp's device-memory pool structure and reserves before it
allocates too. A guest cannot name that structure: the protocol decoder
rejects a chain member it does not know.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
